 "literal": "To the eye, ornament is graciousness; that if without, sore as to be perceived it will be.",
 "translation": "Graciousness is the true ornament for the eyes. Without it, they will be considered mere sores.",
 "modern": "கண்களுக்கு உண்மையான அணிகலன் இரக்கமாகும். அந்த இரக்கம் இல்லாவிட்டால், கண்கள் முகத்தில் உள்ள வெறும் புண்கள் என்றே கருதப்படும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet defines the true purpose and beauty of the eyes. The word 'kaṇṇōṭṭam' literally means the flowing of the eyes, which signifies a gracious, compassionate look towards others. The poet states that this graciousness is the only real ornament ('aṇikalam') for the eyes. If the eyes lack this quality, they are not truly eyes but will be perceived merely as sores ('puṇ') on the face. The contrast between a beautiful jewel and a painful sore emphasizes that physical eyes are worthless without inner kindness.",
